 but could be improved by incorporating better working memory mechanisms that prevent context loss during H-module's strategic reasoning phase. Additionally, while the dual-path System 1/System 2 approach is implemented correctly (L-module every step vs H-module every 4 steps), there might be potential for optimizing attention distribution across these systems to enhance performance further without sacrificing complexity constraints. To address limitations and build on strengths: We should implement an improved working memory system that maintains context throughout the reasoning process, rather than just during L-module cycles. This could potentially involve incorporating hierarchical state information into every layer or developing more sophisticated cross-attention mechanisms between attention and reasoning components to ensure continuous flow of relevant data in both directions while still maintaining linear computational complexity for scalability."}}, "full_result": {"_type": "Dataclass", "_class": "RunResult", "_module": "agents.result", "data": {"input": "# Experience Synthesis Task\n\n## Experimental Context\n\n### Design Motivation\nLinear-HRM Fusion Research:\nInvestigate optimal integration strategies for combining efficient O(n) linear attention \nwith brain-inspired hierarchical reasoning modules to achieve both computational \nscalability and advanced reasoning capabilities. Focus on attention-reasoning synergies, \nadaptive processing depths, and multi-timescale cognitive architectures.\n\n### Performance Analysis\nHybrid Linear-HRM Architecture Performance Analysis:\n- Successfully combines O(n) linear attention with hierarchical reasoning\n- Multi-timescale processing with System 1/System 2 dual-path architecture  \n- Cross-modal fusion enables synergies between attention and reasoning\n- Reasoning accuracy improved over pure attention models\n- Maintains linear computational complexity for scalability\n- Multi-layer hierarchical state management f